# Credentials -- never committed.
.env
*.key
*.pem
# Build it Better request tokens, ticket -> token (scripts/bb.py). Each is shown once, in the
# 201 that files the request, and lets its holder accept, change, judge or withdraw that ticket.
state/bb_tokens.json

# Rebuildable projection of the journal. The journal is the source of truth.
state/qa.sqlite
state/qa.sqlite-*

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# The journal's append lock. A zero-byte file whose only job is to exist while
# one process holds it; committing it would put a machine's transient state in
# everyone's tree.
state/**/*.lock
state/*.lock

# Run records. Every pytest invocation writes one, including the single-file
# runs with --tb=line that make up most of a working session: on 2026-09-07,
# 237 of 283 committed records had adjudicated nothing, and the tree grew
# 9-15 MB on a working day. The cost that mattered was not the megabytes --
# an untracked record makes `git status --porcelain` dirty, prereg.py refuses
# a dirty tree, and so every sweep opened by committing debug output.
#
# A record is evidence when a fold stood on it, and scripts/ledger.py force-
# adds exactly that one. tests/repo asserts the converse: nothing cited by the
# ledger, the journal or a preregistration may be untracked. Records committed
# before this line stay committed.
#
# Since 2026-09-10 two things keep the untracked pile down: a session that ran
# only tests/meta and tests/repo writes no record at all (qa/report_plugin.py
# ::should_record), and scripts/prune_runs.py removes what nothing cites.
state/runs/

# Half-written snapshots. Every atomic write under state/ goes tmp-then-replace,
# and a process killed between the two leaves the tmp behind -- untracked, which
# means git status is dirty, which means prereg.py refuses and no run can reach
# level V. protect_state.py then blocks removing it from a shell, so the
# operator is left with a tree they cannot clean by the sanctioned route.
state/**/*.tmp
state/*.tmp

# The same two for the evidence manifest (qa/manifest.py): its writers -- the
# pre-commit hook, every fold, `keep.py archive` -- take audits/evidence-manifest
# .jsonl.lock and replace the file through a temporary of their own. Untracked
# beside it, either would make every tree dirty and every preregistration refuse.
audits/*.lock
audits/*.tmp

# What Nexus was asked and how long it took to answer. A deep investigation
# runs for minutes and this file grows while it polls, so committing it would
# mean a tree that is dirty exactly while a question is outstanding -- and a
# preregistration refuses a dirty tree. tests/mcp/test_nexus_channel.py reads
# it from disk; nothing else needs it in git.
state/nexus.jsonl

# The same, for his answers through the sync door (qa-agent/ask) while the deep
# lane is at capacity. Its own file because #85 and #86 measure the deep
# channel off nexus.jsonl.
state/nexus_shallow.jsonl

# Every paper trade and the account either side of it. Gitignored for the same
# reason as state/nexus.jsonl -- it is appended to while a campaign runs, and a
# tree that is dirty during a run is a tree no run can reach level V from.
# scripts/paper.py proof reads it from disk.
state/paper.jsonl

# Every paper clone, transfer and pause, and the account either side of them
# (scripts/clone.py). Committed until 2026-09-23, while the laboratory wrote it
# in the middle of a tick -- the same dirty-tree reason as paper.jsonl. Copied
# into audits/lab/ by `scripts/keep.py snapshot`; untracked once by
# `scripts/keep.py release clones.jsonl`.
state/clones.jsonl

# The Nexus rooms: the operator's notes from the console and what the sampler
# reads on a timer. The same reason again, only sharper -- a note is one click
# away at any moment, including the middle of a sweep. qa/rooms.py reads both
# from disk; docs/nexus-rooms-plan.md says why they exist.
state/rooms.jsonl
state/samples.jsonl
# A sampler started as a process of its own once wrote its stdout here (2026-10-03); a
# running process now logs outside the repository, and these two are kept out of the tree.
state/sampler-run.log
state/sampler-run.err

# How long each real request to their host took (qa/timings.py), one line per
# request, written during every run -- tracked, it would cost each run its
# level V. Read by scripts/timeouts.py.
state/timings.jsonl
# The request brake's shared slots (qa/ratelimit.py), rewritten on every request.
state/pace.json

# The laboratory's own notebook: what a session did, what it learned, what the
# next one should pick up. Outside git for the reason the four above are --
# it is appended to while a run may be in flight -- and copied into audits/lab/
# by `scripts/keep.py snapshot`, so months of it are not on one disk.
state/lab.jsonl

# The laboratory's report (`lab.py report`, plan v2 V4): a projection of the
# diaries above, rewritten whenever the /lab page is asked for. Outside git for
# their reason, and so the committed console hashes nothing that is not in git.
state/lab/

# The Gem Screener audit (scripts/gem_audit.py, a department): its diary and its
# report. Written by a tick while a sweep may be in flight, so tracked it would
# cost the run its level V; `keep.py snapshot` copies the diary into audits/.
state/gem_audit.jsonl
state/gem_audit/

# Where the loop's last tick stopped: the search queue and the clocks. Rewritten
# every twenty minutes while a /loop runs, and rebuilt from the diaries if it is
# lost -- a cache of the walk, not a record of anything.
state/lab_tick.json
# Present only while a tick runs (scripts/lab.py tick_exclusive). Committed
# mid-tick it would make a clean tree dirty, and a level-V run impossible.
state/lab_tick.running
# When each running room's index was last read for an early verdict.
state/lab_early.json
# What the last surface scan found (processes.py surface_drift), for the
# session start to read instead of scanning (plan v2, V7). A cache.
state/surface_drift.json

# What the laboratory has already asked their backtest, keyed on the basket.
# Appended to while a run may be in flight, like the diaries above; the deep
# pass copies it into audits/lab/ so months of search do not live on one disk.
state/backtests.jsonl

# FOMO Radar's `hot` signals, tracked to measure whether they actually predict
# a memecoin moving -- scripts/fomo_watch.py. Same reason as the diaries
# above: appended to by a running poller, snapshotted by the deep pass.
state/fomo_signals.jsonl

# The outsider, the laboratory's section 6 (scripts/outsider.py): a stranger agent's runs,
# its picks and its coins' checkpoints. Written by a laboratory tick, so tracked it would
# cost a run its level V; `keep.py snapshot` copies it into audits/lab/.
state/outsider.jsonl

# The company (docs/crew-plan.md, scripts/crew.py): what its loops did, the ticket bus,
# what the manager says to the operator, and its small per-loop files (heartbeats, the
# worker a department is running, logs, the stop flag). Written by detached loops while a
# sweep may be in flight, so tracked they would cost a run its level V; the three diaries
# are a department's, so `keep.py snapshot` copies them into audits/lab/.
state/crew.jsonl
state/crew_tickets.jsonl
state/crew_operator.jsonl
state/crew/

# The Hot headlines watch -- scripts/hot_watch.py, a round every tick. Unlike
# the diaries above it is NOT snapshotted: about 2 MB a day would grow git for
# ever. The six-hourly reading goes into lab.jsonl, which is.
state/hot_watch.jsonl

# Captured requests and responses. 7 747 files and 469 MB by 2026-09-12,
# growing 40-170 MB a day -- 120 MB of one day's 170 written by two checks that
# walk the event-card listing page by page on every run -- and nothing in the
# pipeline reads the store by machine.
#
# What a clone still has to be able to open is force-added and stays tracked:
# their answers the ledger stands on, and the request and response behind every
# verdict and report we have sent, which scripts/verify.py promises them
# "provided on request". qa/keepset.py names that set, .githooks/pre-commit
# adds it in the commit that cites it, scripts/prune_runs.py cuts the rest
# after three days, and tests/repo/test_evidence_in_git.py holds both
# directions -- cited is tracked, and nothing else is.
evidence/

# The monthly evidence bundles (docs/data-plan.md D2). The MANIFEST is in git
# and proves what each one holds; the bodies themselves are what left.
archive/

# The /company mockup's downloaded textures and models (CC0, ambientCG and Poly Haven): the bodies stay out
# of git, outbox/company-live/assets.json (in git) names each file with its source, licence, bytes and sha256.
outbox/company-live/assets/

# Pictures our own scripts render from a page in git (render.py, make_mockup.py, shoot_previews.py, the station
# agents' shots): 97 MB of them sat untracked on 2026-10-25, the pack is 209 MB against its 150 ceiling and the
# 2026-10-18 pass found each mockup picture committed up to six times. Each is remade by running its script; what a
# request text links (a spec/ folder, a picture named in request.txt) is committed by name and is not listed here.
# Exact paths, or a name pattern inside one folder of shots; never a repo-wide pattern like *-1440.png, which would
# silently keep out the next picture a request links.
outbox/company-skins/built-*.png
outbox/company-skins/settings-*.png
outbox/company-skins/showcase-*.png
outbox/company-skins/preview.png
outbox/company-promo-video/shots/
outbox/company-promo-video/shots-overview.png
outbox/company-flyer/flyer-1080x1350.png
outbox/revenue-share-flyer/flyer-1080x1350.png
outbox/revenue-share-flyer/slogan-v*.png
outbox/gem-screener-tiers/tiers-*.png
outbox/gem-screener-cards/card-1440.png
outbox/gem-screener-cards/cards-1440.png
outbox/gem-screener-cards/cards-before-1440.png
outbox/build-it-better-redesign/index.html
outbox/build-it-better-redesign/catalog-1920.png
outbox/build-it-better-redesign/first-screen-1920.png
outbox/build-it-better-redesign/page-1440.png
outbox/build-it-better-redesign/previews/
outbox/event-card-redesign/index.html
